 around the late 19th century  science  began to transform and professionalize  iron working  in that process many african americans  were left out of the picture they were  being excluded and discriminated against  in the workplace  they still played a critical role and  their labor and their expertise was  really important to  the rise of many different industries  today blacksmithing has become an  important art form and african americans  have a very important role in the  contemporary community of artisans  they have persisted in part because they  stayed longer in rural communities  so african-american blacksmiths have  really  remained critical to the persistence of  these techniques of forging by hand  exquisitely beautiful objects out of  iron  [Music]  so next i'll be grinding the knife to  shape  i take a template and i lay it on to the  billet and then now i'm cutting the  excess away so basically the whole  perimeter of the knife  [Music]  [Applause]  after i cut the excess away now it's  time to normalize it and heat treat it  we did all this hard work in the  beginning to get the width and the  thickness we want so it has a lot of  stress inside of it so i need to  basically massage  basically you need to go into the sauna  so that's what normalizing is heating it  to its critical temperature and for this  instance is about 1500 degrees  and then i have to quickly dip it in oil  this process is called quenching  this process is allowing the grain  structure inside the steel to realign  and mesh together and i need it to stay  that way so that's what the quenching  process does  now since i quenched it in oil this  knife is so brittle that i can take a  hammer or throw it on the ground it will  shatter in a thousand pieces  so now i need to put in the oven at 400  degrees for two hours span allowing it  to soften just slightly but still be  hard enough to hold an edge and that is  the key point of  a knife it needs to be able to hold an  edge and stay sharp for a very long time  as a child i was very adventurous i was  a loner  i would play in the woods make my own  bow and arrows sling shots i would take  tubing off my swing set and flatten it  with a hammer and make my own sword  there are not many  probably five or six black knife makers  that i can think of  right now  and it was very hard  because it's a white dominated area and  a lot of people didn't want to teach me  starting out  [Music]  i worked at a local mall and i sold  knives and cigars and swords and a man  came in one day his name is jason knight  clinton was behind the counter and he  knew what he was talking about he knew  all the knives he knew what they were  made out of he knew where they were from  he was very interested in the whole idea  of making knives  and he started telling me that he make  knives for a living and as a 17 year old  boy like my eyes like brighten up like  can you teach me  he's making me nervous  i'm a teacher  gotta make sure i do it right  he'd come to my shop and he would show  me what he made and i would give him  some pointers and then he would go back  and work on that again  and uh one day he  [Music]  he came over and he said god told me to  make chef's knives  i was following suit behind jason making  hunting knives swords and the fantasy  movie swords  and then i had a dream  i'm very big in my faith  and the holy spirit told me plainly make  chef knives  and i woke up i think  that makes sense  probably within eight months every chef  in charleston had a chef's knife in  their hands that quentin had the guts to  go out and say i want to let you try  this knife out and see what you think  and he built a whole business around  that  that's the exciting thing that's the fun  thing is to watch something grow from a  little seed and watch it become awesome  i just i love the guy he's my friend  he's my brother i want him to be the  rock star knife maker that he is  so now next what we'll be doing we  finish uh doing the bevel grinding  basically getting it to a sharp point  so i'm grinding it from the thickness  that it is now to basically down to zero  on the edge  i don't measure anything so it's all  about field i've been doing this 18  years and it's muscle memory i'm  constantly checking it making sure that  my grinds are straight and consistent uh  make sure that everything flows well  and then i will be putting it inside a  developer  right now it looks silver so you really  don't see the the patterns yet until i  put it in the spheric chloride  now you're about to see what we all  wanted to see the pattern the twists and  the turns and the curls and the swooshes  and  doesn't it look sexy  now here comes the heavy part for me  adding the handle  the handle material is going to be  oak but what's so special about this oak  this oak came from a plantation this  plantation is called middleton's place  we are currently  at middleton's place plantation  maybe 20 minutes away from downtown  charleston  and my last name is middleton so  you know the deal  [Music]  my ancestors were here  they  worked this land  they bled here they died here  they were beaten here they may were hung  here  this oak tree is basically a live oak or  enchanted angel oak  this tree has been here since slavery  time  the directors over middleton place  plantation  we had this little project i made some  oyster nights for them and they were  kind enough to gift me some of the wood  that fell off this tree  and that's the handler i use for this  knife  what i want to do  is build off broken pieces  i'm taking something that's  barbaric that's  a stain on america's history  and building off of that in my own way  this wood is basically a block and i'm  going to cut it to shape  i want to create a chemical and a  mechanical bond the chemical bond is the  epoxy  the mechanical bond is a screw and i can  screw the handle to the blade  after we let the glue dry we will grind  the perimeter of the handle  basically i'm just trying to get that  coke bottle shape i just want it to be  smooth i want it to be almost like a car  the lines need to fit they need to flow  just right  and i want to add a sealer  now you're about to see the rings of the  oak you can almost count and see how old  this tree really was  now it's time to sharpen the knife and  i'm paying very close attention on the  angle making sure that it's not too  steep or not too shallow  now you're seeing how sexy this knife  looked i am proud to put my name on it  so now i'm going to be putting my makers  mark on it my logo i use a laser  engraver and we'll be allowing the laser  to engrave my logo inside this knife  growing up  my granddad told me your name is  everything  to put your name on something that means  you stand by it just like your handshake  you stand by your handshake  [Music]  now it's time to box it up  wrap it up and ship it out  since this one is so special and this is  so much emotion inside this one  it'll probably be around two thousand  dollars  [Music]  people ask me a lot how long does it  take to make a knife  i answered that two ways  if i have everything  on hand i can make that knife in eight  hours  and the second way i answer that is kind  of snobbish  18 years  and you got you have to look off at it  [Laughter]  the time and the mistakes that i made  and all the blood sweat and tears i put  into my craft it feels like second  nature to me  [Music]  growing up as a boy you never really  hear like you're doing a good job  as a southern man you more like go to  school go to work work hard provide for  your family  but to build this knife in honor of my  ancestors they're saying job well done  i've built my brain i've built this  company  and what i'm hearing in my spirit what  i'm hearing in my inner man is saying  i'm proud of you  that's what i feel  [Music]  you

